// [DA] One fixed step of the water standing on the actor's visor.
//
// The effect this replaces drew a fixed lattice of 48x36 cells, hashed each cell to a constant,
// and showed the cell as a drop when that constant passed a threshold the rain driver moved once
// a second in steps of three hundredths. Everything the eye complains about follows from that
// sentence: drops arrive in slabs because a threshold crosses a batch of cells at once, they
// never move because nothing in the function is a clock, they never merge because they are cells,
// and a wipe deletes every one of them in the same frame because it sets the threshold to zero.
//
// What is here instead is the water itself, in a target with a memory. The state is
//
//     R = h,     the water's thickness at this texel, millimetres
//     G = film,  what a trail or a wipe left behind, millimetres, always thin
//     BA = v,    the velocity of the water here, millimetres a second, in the plane of the glass
//
// and the four things a drop on a pane actually does all fall out of stepping it:
//
//   * IT STAYS WHERE IT LANDS. A drop on glass is held by contact angle hysteresis - the
//     advancing edge wants a steeper angle than the receding one, and the difference is a force
//     per unit of contact line. Furmidge's relation says it lets go when
//         rho g V sin(a) = k w gamma (cos th_r - cos th_a)
//     so the criterion is a SIZE: below a critical weight the drop is pinned however long you
//     wait, above it the drop runs. That threshold is why a windscreen in light rain holds a
//     field of motionless beads, and it is the single thing no lattice of cells can express.
//
//   * IT GROWS, AND IT SWALLOWS ITS NEIGHBOURS. Rain keeps arriving; two drops that touch are
//     one drop with the sum of their water. Here that is addition, which is what a field is for.
//
//   * ONCE IT RUNS IT LEAVES A TRACK, AND THE NEXT DROP FOLLOWS IT. A sliding drop cannot take
//     all its water with it - it leaves a film microns thick behind it, and that film is already
//     wet, so the next drop to reach it is pinned less hard and veers into it. That is why rain
//     on a window runs in a few channels instead of everywhere.
//
//   * IT MOVES IN JERKS. A drop that runs thins, drops back under the critical size, stops, is
//     fed by the rain until it is over it again, and goes. Nothing here implements stick-slip;
//     it is what the threshold and the trail do together.
//
// HOW THE WATER MOVES is the part that had to be got right three times, and it is worth writing
// down because it is not obvious and it is invisible in a screenshot.
//
// The natural thing to write in a pixel shader is a backward trace: what is standing here now is
// what was one step upstream, so read the state there and take it. It is what the ripple field
// does, and it is stable and cheap. It is also NOT CONSERVATIVE, and on a field of discrete drops
// that is fatal in two stages. First, a texel of dry glass ahead of a drop holds no velocity, so
// it traces back to itself, finds nothing and stays dry - no drop can ever move onto glass that
// is not already wet, and every drop sits exactly where it landed however heavy it gets. Then,
// when the velocity is made to reach past the water so the glass ahead knows what is coming, the
// leading edge starts gaining without the trailing edge losing: a semi-Lagrangian trace preserves
// the value it samples, not the mass, and where the velocity has divergence - which here is
// everywhere, since it is zero on a pinned drop and high on a running one - water is quietly
// created. The rig showed it as the glass filling to half covered while the pattern never shifted
// by a single texel.
//
// So the transport is a FLUX. Each cell gives a share of what it holds to the neighbour its own
// velocity points at, and takes the shares its neighbours send it; what one gives is exactly what
// another gets, so nothing is created and nothing is lost. The price is the Courant condition -
// water may not cross more than one cell in a step - and it is paid by stepping at 360 Hz rather
// than by pretending it does not exist.

#include "common.h"

Texture2D s_visor_prev;

// x = the step in seconds, y = millimetres of glass one texel covers, z = the step number (the
// spawn hash's clock), w = how hard the glass is being wetted right now, 0..1.
uniform float4 da_visor_sim;
// xy = which way is down IN THE PLANE OF THE GLASS, unit; z = how much of gravity the plate
// feels, 0 when the visor is horizontal and 1 when it faces the horizon; w = the target's aspect.
uniform float4 da_visor_grav;
// x = 1 while a hand is going across, y = how far across it is 0..1, z = which way it travels,
// w = the radius in mm of a lens the rig holds at the centre of the glass, 0 for none.
uniform float4 da_visor_wipe;
// xy = one texel in uv, zw = the target's size in texels.
uniform float4 da_visor_dim;

//	The thickness at which a drop stops being pinned, millimetres, measured over the disc below
//	and not under one pixel. The criterion is a Bond number - the drop's weight against the
//	retention of its own contact line - and it crosses one at a radius of the capillary length
//	times the root of the critical Bond number: sqrt(gamma / rho g) = 2.728 mm, Bo_c between 0.15
//	and 0.35 on glass, so 1.1 to 1.6 mm. That agrees with the measurements - five to fifteen
//	microlitres pipetted onto a glass slide is a radius of 1.1 to 1.5 mm, and that is where drops
//	begin to run. Divided by the share of gravity the plate feels, so a visor tipped back toward
//	the sky holds drops it would let go of when level.
//
//	It also decides how WET the glass gets, which is not obvious and is what set this number in
//	the end. Water leaves the visor two ways, by drying and by running off the bottom, and the
//	second dwarfs the first - so the equilibrium is set by how readily a drop lets go, not by how
//	hard it is raining. At a third of a millimetre a lone bead the size of a grain of rice was
//	already running, everything drained as fast as it arrived, and the glass held a twentieth of
//	what a photograph of a rainy window holds however much rain was thrown at it. Against that,
//	it has to sit UNDER the bead height with capillarity on: a bead now stands at that height
//	and no taller, and if the threshold matches it nothing short of a wide merged pancake ever
//	runs - one run in a hundred cells of water, and a glass with no tracks on it.
#define DA_VD_PIN		0.40f
//	Once it is moving it needs only this share of that - the receding angle is not the advancing
//	one, and the difference is the whole of contact angle hysteresis. Big enough that a running
//	drop does not stall the moment it thins, and no bigger: at a third of the static threshold
//	nothing that ever started running stopped again, the glass drained as fast as it filled, and
//	what was left was a set of channels with no beads between them.
#define DA_VD_PIN_GO	0.62f
//	The radius the weight is measured over, millimetres: a drop's own footprint. Two drops that
//	merged into a wide low patch weigh what a tall one weighs and have to run like it, and the
//	thickness under one pixel cannot tell the difference.
#define DA_VD_WEIGH_MM	1.60f
//	Terminal speed per millimetre of weighed thickness over the threshold, mm/s. A drop on a pane
//	runs at ten to thirty centimetres a second - a 22 cm visor in one to two seconds - and reaches
//	that speed within a few millimetres of travel, which at this step is instantly.
#define DA_VD_SPEED		420.0f
//	The Courant number: the share of a cell water may cross in one step. Past one the flux would
//	have to send more water than a cell holds and the scheme stops conserving; just under one is
//	also where an upwind flux stops SMEARING, because its numerical diffusion goes as
//	CFL (1 - CFL) and vanishes at both ends. At 0.8 a drop spread to twice its width in a second
//	of running and arrived at the bottom of the glass as a streak with no head; at 0.95 the same
//	drop keeps its shape.
#define DA_VD_CFL		0.95f
//	How fast the water gets to its speed and back, 1/s. Capillary flow, so: quickly.
#define DA_VD_DRAG		25.0f
//	What a moving drop leaves behind, as a share of the water that crossed out of the cell, and
//	the ceiling on the film that builds from it. The real trail is single-digit microns - far
//	under one texel of anything - so the number is a look and not a measurement, but it is bounded
//	from above by something real: a running drop crosses its own width many times over, and at a
//	seventh of what leaves each cell it had paid out nine tenths of itself within a third of a
//	second. It arrived at the bottom of the glass as a stain with no drop at the end of it, which
//	is not what a trail is.
#define DA_VD_TRAIL		0.012f
#define DA_VD_FILM_MAX	0.060f
//	How hard water veers toward the wet side. Pure gravity gives parallel tracks; this is what
//	makes a window run in a few channels - but it is a positive feedback, and too much of it combs
//	the whole plate into evenly spaced parallel stripes. A window has a few irregular channels and
//	a lot of beads, not corduroy.
#define DA_VD_FOLLOW	0.12f
//	SURFACE TENSION, as millimetres a second per unit of surface slope: the speed the water on a
//	flank relaxes to, the same way it relaxes to its running speed under gravity. It was added to
//	the velocity every step instead, which made it an acceleration - one balanced only by the
//	drag, at a speed that came out at slope times 1500 mm/s, saturating the Courant limit for any
//	slope at all and depending on the step length. Every drop assembled itself at full speed and
//	overshot: the momentum outlived the brake by forty milliseconds and kept flowing.
//
//	Water on a plate does not spread - it DEWETS. Thin water drains into thick, which is why a
//	window in the rain is a field of round beads and a few narrow channels rather than an even
//	sheet, and why every bead has a rim. Without it the field only ever transports and diffuses,
//	so drops arrive as beads and immediately relax into wide flat smears: the reference the player
//	held up against it was a photograph of a window with distinct beads on it, and what the field
//	had were streaks.
//
//	This is the second attempt at the term. The first moved MASS directly - thin cells gave to
//	thick ones - which is anti-diffusion, and the mode anti-diffusion grows fastest is the
//	checkerboard: every drop filled with crawling speckle within a second. Here the same tendency
//	is a VELOCITY, pointing up the thickness gradient, and it is carried by the same conservative
//	flux as gravity is: nothing can be created, nothing can outrun the Courant limit, and the
//	gradient is read over two cells so that a checkerboard - which has none over that distance -
//	cannot drive it at all.
//
//	It stops at a height rather than running away into a spike, and that height is a real number:
//	a drop on a plate stands about a millimetre tall whatever its volume, because that is where
//	its own weight balances the curvature its contact angle allows. Past it the pull is off.
#define DA_VD_TENSION	25.0f
//	The height surface tension pulls to, and above which the water SPREADS again. It is also, for
//	a given amount of water on the glass, the thing that decides how much of the glass is
//	COVERED: the same water in beads half as tall wets twice the area. Half a millimetre is what a
//	small bead stands at on a plate that is not freshly waxed, and it is what makes the plate read
//	as rained-on rather than as a few beads on bare glass.
#define DA_VD_BEAD_H	0.55f
//	How fast what stands above that height spreads to its neighbours, as a share per step. A
//	pull that only ever climbs has no way back down: once a cell overshot the height, nothing
//	took the surplus off it, and on a fine grid every bead became a crater - the flank kept
//	receiving from the cells behind it after the brake had stopped it sending on, and stood
//	taller than the middle. A quarter is the explicit limit for four neighbours; this is under it.
#define DA_VD_SPREAD	0.10f
//	CAPILLARITY: the flow the surface's own curvature drives, mm^4/s. The pressure under a water
//	surface is its curvature times the surface tension, so water flows from where the surface is
//	convex to where it is concave, and the shape that flow leaves alone is the one with the same
//	curvature everywhere: a spherical cap. It is what makes a drop ROUND - a finger sticking out
//	of a bead is a ridge of high curvature and is drawn back in; a plateau with a cell-wide edge
//	is a cap within a tenth of a second - and without it the climb above, which is a scalar and
//	knows nothing of shape, grew the beads as staircases along the grid's own axes. The mobility
//	is the water's, so it goes to zero on dry glass: the flow rounds and smooths a drop but cannot
//	spread it onto glass it does not already wet. Physically the coefficient is gamma h^3 / 3 mu,
//	which for a half-millimetre of water is of the order of ten mm^4/s; a thousandth of that is
//	taken, because the real figure rounds a two-millimetre bead in a millisecond and would have
//	to be stepped a thousand times finer to be stable. What is here rounds a millimetre in a
//	fifth of a second and two in a few, which is the timescale a player can see.
#define DA_VD_CAPILLARY	0.0032f
//	The thickness at which glass counts as wet for the mobility, millimetres.
#define DA_VD_MOBILE_MM	0.08f
//	Drying, mm/s, in DRY AIR: a drop is minutes, the film a few seconds. Neither happens while it
//	rains - the air over a plate in the rain is saturated - and that is applied below, not here.
#define DA_VD_DRY		0.006f
#define DA_VD_DRY_FILM	0.020f
//	Drops per square metre per second at full rate. Marshall-Palmer at 25 mm/h gives a few thousand
//	drops per cubic metre and a fall speed of several metres a second, so a plate facing the
//	horizon catches thousands per square metre per second - and it has to, because everything
//	downstream depends on drops MEETING each other. It also has to be enough water to LOOK like
//	rain: a photograph of a rainy window carries a few tenths of a millimetre of water averaged
//	over the glass, and once surface tension pulls that into beads a millimetre tall it covers a
//	third of the plate. Spread thin it covered a third at a tenth of the water, which is how the
//	field looked wet while holding almost nothing - and why every shape on it was a smear. Taken
//	down by a quarter from there at the player's request: at the full figure the glass closed
//	over too fast to see past in a downpour, and a visor is a thing you look THROUGH.
#define DA_VD_FLUX		22500.0f
//	The cell the spawn lottery is drawn on, in millimetres: bigger than the drops, or two of them
//	land in the same place.
#define DA_VD_CELL_MM	4.0f
//	Radius of an arriving drop, millimetres: the small ones are most of them, and they have to be
//	small. A drop that arrives already near the critical radius runs almost at once and takes its
//	water off the bottom of the glass with it, so a spectrum reaching two millimetres drains the
//	visor as fast as it wets it and leaves a few channels on bare glass. What a photograph of a
//	rainy window shows is mostly beads well under a millimetre that have been sitting there for
//	a while, with the occasional merged one running.
#define DA_VD_RAD_MIN	0.28f
#define DA_VD_RAD_MAX	1.15f
//	The ceiling. Whatever the shape regime, a drop on a pane stays about a millimetre tall - past
//	that it stops being taller and starts being longer, and it is already running. Set clear of
//	the height surface tension pulls to, because everything over it is thrown away and thrown-away
//	water is the one way this scheme can lose mass.
#define DA_VD_CLAMP		2.40f

//	The hand. It does not delete water - it COLLECTS it: the edge drives everything it touches
//	forward faster than it is itself travelling, so the water piles into a bead that rides ahead of
//	it and runs off the far side. Only what the bead could not take is left behind, and it is left
//	as a smeared film and not as clean glass.
#define DA_VD_WIPE_W	11.0f
#define DA_VD_WIPE_PUSH	30.0f
#define DA_VD_WIPE_KEEP	0.10f
#define DA_VD_WIPE_SMEAR 0.85f

float da_vd_hash(int2 p, uint n)
{
	uint h = uint(p.x) * 73856093u ^ uint(p.y) * 19349663u ^ n * 83492791u;
	h ^= h >> 13;
	h *= 0x5bd1e995u;
	h ^= h >> 15;
	return float(h & 0x00ffffffu) * (1.0f / 16777216.0f);
}

//	A smooth field of how rough the glass is here: where the plate holds a drop harder than average
//	and where it lets one go early. Two millimetres across, so it is per drop and not per texel - a
//	per-texel threshold is noise, and noise makes a field crawl instead of run.
float da_vd_pinning(float2 mm)
{
	const float2 c = mm * (1.0f / 2.2f);
	const int2 i = int2(floor(c));
	const float2 f = c - float2(i);
	const float2 w = f * f * (3.0f - 2.0f * f);
	const float a = da_vd_hash(i, 7u), b = da_vd_hash(i + int2(1, 0), 7u);
	const float d = da_vd_hash(i + int2(0, 1), 7u), e = da_vd_hash(i + int2(1, 1), 7u);
	return lerp(lerp(a, b, w.x), lerp(d, e, w.x), w.y);
}

struct v2p
{
	float2	tc0		: TEXCOORD0;
};

//	Point-sampled: this is a finite-volume scheme, and a texel is a cell with a mass in it rather
//	than a sample of a smooth function. Interpolating between cells would move water no flux ever
//	sent, which is exactly the leak the transport is written this way to close.
float4 da_vd_read(float2 uv)
{
	return s_visor_prev.SampleLevel(smp_nofilter, uv, 0);
}

//	What a cell sends this step, as shares of what it holds: fx along x and fy along y, each in
//	the direction of its own velocity and each held under the Courant number on its own. What
//	actually leaves is fx (1 - fy) to the x neighbour, fy (1 - fx) to the y neighbour, fx fy to
//	the CORNER between them, and (1 - fx)(1 - fy) stays - corner transport upwind. The corner
//	share is the whole point. Sent along the axes only, a drop running diagonally leaks a share
//	sideways at every cell it crosses, and the leak compounds: after a hundred cells the drop is
//	a triangle ten cells wide with the drop's own water spread over it a few microns deep. That
//	is what the storm did to the glass - the wind lays every track over, so every track ran
//	diagonally, and every one became a cone that widened as it fell, thinned past seeing, and
//	ran off, until the glass held nothing visible however hard it rained. With the corner share
//	a diagonal run is carried exactly at the Courant limit and the leak is gone.
float2 da_vd_outflow(float2 v, float k)
{
	return min(abs(v) * k, DA_VD_CFL);
}

float4 main(v2p I) : SV_Target
{
	const float dt = da_visor_sim.x;
	const float texel_mm = da_visor_sim.y;
	//	Not "step": that is an HLSL intrinsic, and a local of the same name hides it for the rest
	//	of the function - which the border guard below needs.
	const uint step_n = uint(da_visor_sim.z);
	const float wet = da_visor_sim.w;
	const float2 gdir = da_visor_grav.xy;
	const float gshare = da_visor_grav.z;

	//	The target carries the screen's aspect, so a texel is square in millimetres and one number
	//	does for each axis - which is the whole reason it is not a square target.
	const float2 texel_uv = da_visor_dim.xy;
	const float2 uv_per_mm = texel_uv / texel_mm;
	const float2 mm_here = I.tc0 / max(uv_per_mm, 1e-6f);
	//	Millimetres a second, turned into a share of a cell per step.
	const float k_flux = dt / texel_mm;
	//	The fastest the Courant condition allows, in millimetres a second.
	const float v_max = DA_VD_CFL / max(k_flux, 1e-6f);

	//	---- transport: a flux, so that mass is conserved --------------------------------------------
	//	Each of the nine cells is asked what it sends where, from the velocity it was holding. What
	//	this cell keeps plus what its neighbours send it is what it now has. Momentum rides along
	//	with the mass, so a drop keeps its speed as it crosses cells and a slow drop swallowed by a
	//	fast one comes out at the weighted speed of the two - which is coalescence.
	const float4 c = da_vd_read(I.tc0);
	const float4 xm = da_vd_read(I.tc0 - float2(texel_uv.x, 0.0f));
	const float4 xp = da_vd_read(I.tc0 + float2(texel_uv.x, 0.0f));
	const float4 ym = da_vd_read(I.tc0 - float2(0.0f, texel_uv.y));
	const float4 yp = da_vd_read(I.tc0 + float2(0.0f, texel_uv.y));
	//	The corners: they send their corner share, and they carry the curvature stencil's ring.
	const float4 dmm = da_vd_read(I.tc0 - texel_uv);
	const float4 dpp = da_vd_read(I.tc0 + texel_uv);
	const float4 dpm = da_vd_read(I.tc0 + float2(texel_uv.x, -texel_uv.y));
	const float4 dmp = da_vd_read(I.tc0 + float2(-texel_uv.x, texel_uv.y));

	//	The second ring, for the curvature: the Laplacian at each neighbour needs the cells past it.
	const float xmm = da_vd_read(I.tc0 - float2(2.0f * texel_uv.x, 0.0f)).x;
	const float xpp = da_vd_read(I.tc0 + float2(2.0f * texel_uv.x, 0.0f)).x;
	const float ymm = da_vd_read(I.tc0 - float2(0.0f, 2.0f * texel_uv.y)).x;
	const float ypp = da_vd_read(I.tc0 + float2(0.0f, 2.0f * texel_uv.y)).x;

	const float2 fc = da_vd_outflow(c.zw, k_flux);
	const float2 fxm = da_vd_outflow(xm.zw, k_flux);
	const float2 fxp = da_vd_outflow(xp.zw, k_flux);
	const float2 fym = da_vd_outflow(ym.zw, k_flux);
	const float2 fyp = da_vd_outflow(yp.zw, k_flux);
	const float2 fmm = da_vd_outflow(dmm.zw, k_flux);
	const float2 fpp = da_vd_outflow(dpp.zw, k_flux);
	const float2 fpm = da_vd_outflow(dpm.zw, k_flux);
	const float2 fmp = da_vd_outflow(dmp.zw, k_flux);

	//	Only a neighbour whose velocity points AT this cell sends it anything - an edge neighbour
	//	its axis share if the one component points here, a corner its corner share if both do -
	//	and only if it is a neighbour at all. A sample off the edge of the target comes back
	//	clamped, which is this cell reading itself: on the top row, where the water above would be
	//	flowing down into it, that is a cell receiving a share of its own water every step and the
	//	row slowly filling with water nothing ever sent. Water that runs off the bottom is simply
	//	gone, which is correct - it has left the glass.
	const float2 lo = step(0.0f, I.tc0 - texel_uv);
	const float2 hi = step(I.tc0 + texel_uv, 1.0f);
	const float in_xm = (xm.z > 0.0f) ? fxm.x * (1.0f - fxm.y) * lo.x : 0.0f;
	const float in_xp = (xp.z < 0.0f) ? fxp.x * (1.0f - fxp.y) * hi.x : 0.0f;
	const float in_ym = (ym.w > 0.0f) ? fym.y * (1.0f - fym.x) * lo.y : 0.0f;
	const float in_yp = (yp.w < 0.0f) ? fyp.y * (1.0f - fyp.x) * hi.y : 0.0f;
	const float in_mm = (dmm.z > 0.0f && dmm.w > 0.0f) ? fmm.x * fmm.y * lo.x * lo.y : 0.0f;
	const float in_pp = (dpp.z < 0.0f && dpp.w < 0.0f) ? fpp.x * fpp.y * hi.x * hi.y : 0.0f;
	const float in_pm = (dpm.z < 0.0f && dpm.w > 0.0f) ? fpm.x * fpm.y * hi.x * lo.y : 0.0f;
	const float in_mp = (dmp.z > 0.0f && dmp.w < 0.0f) ? fmp.x * fmp.y * lo.x * hi.y : 0.0f;

	const float keep = (1.0f - fc.x) * (1.0f - fc.y);
	float h = c.x * keep + xm.x * in_xm + xp.x * in_xp + ym.x * in_ym + yp.x * in_yp +
		dmm.x * in_mm + dpp.x * in_pp + dpm.x * in_pm + dmp.x * in_mp;
	const float2 mom = c.x * c.zw * keep + xm.x * xm.zw * in_xm + xp.x * xp.zw * in_xp +
		ym.x * ym.zw * in_ym + yp.x * yp.zw * in_yp + dmm.x * dmm.zw * in_mm +
		dpp.x * dpp.zw * in_pp + dpm.x * dpm.zw * in_pm + dmp.x * dmp.zw * in_mp;
	float2 v = (h > 1e-5f) ? (mom / h) : (float2)0.0f;
	//	The film is what was LEFT BEHIND, so it belongs to the glass and not to the water: it is
	//	read from this cell and never carried with the flux.
	float film = c.y;

	//	---- the trail --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	//	The water that moved on did not take all of itself: a sliding drop leaves microns behind, and
	//	that film is what the next drop follows.
	film = min(film + max(c.x - h, 0.0f) * DA_VD_TRAIL, DA_VD_FILM_MAX);

	//	---- the footprint ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
	//	Eight taps on a ring of the critical radius. The sliding criterion is a WEIGHT, so what has to
	//	be weighed is the water within a drop's own footprint and not the millimetre under this one
	//	pixel: a wide merged patch and a tall single drop then read alike, which is the point.
	float h_weigh = h;
	{
		const float2 r1 = uv_per_mm * DA_VD_WEIGH_MM;
		const float2 d1 = r1 * 0.7071f;
		float ring = da_vd_read(I.tc0 + float2(r1.x, 0.0f)).x;
		ring += da_vd_read(I.tc0 - float2(r1.x, 0.0f)).x;
		ring += da_vd_read(I.tc0 + float2(0.0f, r1.y)).x;
		ring += da_vd_read(I.tc0 - float2(0.0f, r1.y)).x;
		ring += da_vd_read(I.tc0 + d1).x;
		ring += da_vd_read(I.tc0 - d1).x;
		ring += da_vd_read(I.tc0 + float2(d1.x, -d1.y)).x;
		ring += da_vd_read(I.tc0 - float2(d1.x, -d1.y)).x;
		h_weigh = 0.5f * h + 0.5f * (ring * 0.125f);
	}

	//	---- pinning and the pull -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	//	The threshold is a weight, scaled by how much of gravity the plate feels and by how rough the
	//	glass is here; a drop already moving needs less of it, which is the hysteresis itself. Wet
	//	glass holds worse than dry, so a track keeps its channel open.
	const float rough = 0.75f + 0.5f * da_vd_pinning(mm_here);
	const float wetter = 1.0f - saturate(film / DA_VD_FILM_MAX) * 0.35f;
	const float moving = saturate(length(v) * 0.08f);
	const float pin = lerp(1.0f, DA_VD_PIN_GO, moving) * DA_VD_PIN * rough * wetter /
		max(gshare, 0.05f);

	//	Which way it goes: down the glass, veered toward whichever side is wetter. The bias is taken
	//	across gravity, so a track pulls a drop sideways into it and never speeds it up.
	float2 dir = gdir;
	[branch]
	if (h > 0.02f)
	{
		const float2 perp = float2(-gdir.y, gdir.x);
		const float2 probe = perp * (2.5f * uv_per_mm.x);
		const float4 sl = da_vd_read(I.tc0 - probe);
		const float4 sr = da_vd_read(I.tc0 + probe);
		const float wl = sl.y + sl.x * 0.15f;
		const float wr = sr.y + sr.x * 0.15f;
		dir = normalize(gdir + perp * ((wr - wl) * (DA_VD_FOLLOW / max(DA_VD_FILM_MAX, 1e-4f))));
	}

	//	Terminal speed grows with how far over the threshold the drop is: a marginal drop crawls, a
	//	fat one runs. Below the threshold the target is zero and the drag parks it.
	const float over = max(h_weigh - pin, 0.0f);
	float2 target = dir * (min(over * DA_VD_SPEED, v_max) * gshare);

	//	---- surface tension: water climbs into the thick -----------------------------------------
	//	The slope is read over a baseline of HALF A MILLIMETRE each way, in millimetres and not in
	//	cells, and each end is the mean of two cells. Both matter. A pull up the gradient is an
	//	anti-diffusion, and the mode an anti-diffusion grows fastest is the shortest one the stencil
	//	can see: read over two cells it is blind to the checkerboard but drives the four-cell
	//	ripple, and how fast goes as one over the cell size squared - on a finer grid it went from
	//	invisible to a dotted lattice down every track. A baseline fixed in millimetres also makes
	//	the strength of the pull a property of the water rather than of the preset's grid: read
	//	over cells, halving the cell doubled the pull, and the beads on the top tier stood twice as
	//	narrow as on the one below. Braked as the water it is climbing into approaches the height
	//	a drop can stand at, and off entirely where there is no water to move.
	[branch]
	if (h > 0.008f)
	{
		const float b = max(floor(0.5f / texel_mm + 0.5f), 1.0f);
		const float2 eb = texel_uv * b, eb1 = texel_uv * (b + 1.0f);
		const float hx1 = 0.5f * (da_vd_read(I.tc0 + float2(eb.x, 0.0f)).x + da_vd_read(I.tc0 + float2(eb1.x, 0.0f)).x);
		const float hx0 = 0.5f * (da_vd_read(I.tc0 - float2(eb.x, 0.0f)).x + da_vd_read(I.tc0 - float2(eb1.x, 0.0f)).x);
		const float hy1 = 0.5f * (da_vd_read(I.tc0 + float2(0.0f, eb.y)).x + da_vd_read(I.tc0 + float2(0.0f, eb1.y)).x);
		const float hy0 = 0.5f * (da_vd_read(I.tc0 - float2(0.0f, eb.y)).x + da_vd_read(I.tc0 - float2(0.0f, eb1.y)).x);
		//	Braked on what is already standing right here and next door, not two cells away: the
		//	pull has to stop as the water it is climbing into reaches the height a drop can stand
		//	at, and the cell it would climb into is the neighbour, not the one past it. Braked on
		//	the far ring instead, the water kept climbing until it hit the ceiling on thickness -
		//	where the clamp threw the surplus away, and five sixths of the water on the glass went
		//	with it.
		const float here_tall = max(max(max(c.x, xm.x), max(xp.x, ym.x)), yp.x);
		const float brake = saturate((DA_VD_BEAD_H - max(here_tall, h)) / DA_VD_BEAD_H);
		//	Slope of the surface across the baseline, dimensionless.
		const float2 slope = float2(hx1 - hx0, hy1 - hy0) / ((2.0f * b + 1.0f) * texel_mm);
		target += slope * (DA_VD_TENSION * brake * saturate(h * 40.0f));
	}
	v += (target - v) * saturate(DA_VD_DRAG * dt);

	//	What each edge neighbour still holds after the flux, and which faces are real: both the
	//	spread and the capillary flow below scale what a sender gives by the first and close the
	//	border faces with the second.
	const float4 keep_nb = float4((1.0f - fxm.x) * (1.0f - fxm.y), (1.0f - fxp.x) * (1.0f - fxp.y),
		(1.0f - fym.x) * (1.0f - fym.y), (1.0f - fyp.x) * (1.0f - fyp.y));
	const float4 guard = float4(lo.x, hi.x, lo.y, hi.y);

	//	---- and back down: what stands above the bead height spreads ----------------------------
	//	Conservative, like the flux: what a cell sheds its real neighbours receive, and a cell on
	//	the edge sheds only to the neighbours it has. Not under the rig's lens, which is held.
	{
		const float lens = (da_visor_wipe.w > 0.0f) ? 1.0f : 0.0f;
		const float2 dl = (I.tc0 - 0.5f) / max(uv_per_mm, 1e-6f);
		const float held = lens * step(dot(dl, dl), (da_visor_wipe.w + 1.0f) * (da_visor_wipe.w + 1.0f));
		const float shed = DA_VD_SPREAD * (1.0f - held);
		//	Every amount is scaled by what its SENDER still holds after the flux - the sender's own
		//	keep, which both sides can work out from the sender's velocity, so the two sides of a
		//	face still agree. Without it a cell that had just sent nine tenths of itself down the
		//	glass shed a surplus it no longer had, went under, was clamped to nothing, and the clamp
		//	minted the difference: in a storm, with a quarter of the glass running, that flooded the
		//	visor to half a millimetre everywhere - more water than had fallen on it.
		const float ex_c = max(c.x - DA_VD_BEAD_H, 0.0f) * keep;
		const float4 ex_nb = max(float4(xm.x, xp.x, ym.x, yp.x) - DA_VD_BEAD_H, 0.0f) * keep_nb;
		h += (dot(ex_nb, guard) - ex_c * dot(guard, 1.0f)) * shed;
	}

	//	---- capillarity: the surface flows toward the same curvature everywhere ------------------
	//	The Laplacian of the thickness here and at each neighbour, the flow across each face as
	//	the difference of the two times the mobility of that face - the drier of its two cells, so
	//	dry glass takes nothing - and the sum of the four is what this cell loses. Antisymmetric
	//	across every face, so conservative, and the border faces are closed. The coefficient is
	//	turned into a share per step from the cell size and the step, and held under the explicit
	//	limit for a biharmonic stencil. That limit is a THIRTY-SECOND, not a sixteenth: the
	//	five-point Laplacian's eigenvalues reach -8, the biharmonic's their square, and the step
	//	is stable only while the share times sixty-four stays under two. At 0.04 the checkerboard
	//	grew, went negative, was clamped to zero, and the clamp minted water until half the glass
	//	was a millimetre deep.
	{
		const float lens = (da_visor_wipe.w > 0.0f) ? 1.0f : 0.0f;
		const float2 dl = (I.tc0 - 0.5f) / max(uv_per_mm, 1e-6f);
		const float held = lens * step(dot(dl, dl), (da_visor_wipe.w + 1.0f) * (da_visor_wipe.w + 1.0f));
		const float t4 = texel_mm * texel_mm * texel_mm * texel_mm;
		const float k4 = min(DA_VD_CAPILLARY * dt / max(t4, 1e-9f), 0.022f) * (1.0f - held);
		const float lc = xm.x + xp.x + ym.x + yp.x - 4.0f * c.x;
		const float lxm = xmm + c.x + dmm.x + dmp.x - 4.0f * xm.x;
		const float lxp = xpp + c.x + dpm.x + dpp.x - 4.0f * xp.x;
		const float lym = ymm + c.x + dmm.x + dpm.x - 4.0f * ym.x;
		const float lyp = ypp + c.x + dmp.x + dpp.x - 4.0f * yp.x;
		const float4 mob = saturate(min(c.x, float4(xm.x, xp.x, ym.x, yp.x)) * (1.0f / DA_VD_MOBILE_MM));
		//	The same keep as the spread, on whichever side of each face is the one losing water.
		const float4 dlap = float4(lxm, lxp, lym, lyp) - lc;
		const float4 keep_loser = lerp(keep_nb, (float4)keep, step(0.0f, dlap));
		const float flow = dot(mob * dlap * keep_loser * guard, 1.0f);
		h -= k4 * flow;
	}
	//	A cell that ran at the Courant limit AND shed its surplus could go under; the trail below
	//	measures what left by the difference, so it must not.
	h = max(h, 0.0f);

	//	---- the hand ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	//	A sweep, not a switch. The edge crosses the glass over the better part of a second: what it
	//	touches is driven forward as fast as the scheme can carry water, so it piles into a bead in
	//	front of the hand, and what the bead could not take is smeared behind it.
	[branch]
	if (da_visor_wipe.x > 0.5f)
	{
		//	The hand travels across the glass, so along the visor's own x, easing in and out the way
		//	an arm does. -0.15..1.15, so it starts and ends off the plate.
		const float ph = da_visor_wipe.y;
		const float eased = ph * ph * (3.0f - 2.0f * ph);
		const float lane = lerp(-0.15f, 1.15f, (da_visor_wipe.z > 0.0f) ? eased : (1.0f - eased));
		const float2 travel = float2((da_visor_wipe.z > 0.0f) ? 1.0f : -1.0f, 0.0f);
		//	Distance from the edge, in millimetres, signed so that positive is ahead of it.
		const float d_mm = (I.tc0.x - lane) / max(uv_per_mm.x, 1e-6f) * travel.x;

		[branch]
		if (d_mm > -DA_VD_WIPE_W && d_mm < DA_VD_WIPE_PUSH)
		{
			const float k = (d_mm < 0.0f) ? 1.0f : (1.0f - d_mm / DA_VD_WIPE_PUSH);
			v = lerp(v, travel * v_max, saturate(k * k * 0.95f));
		}
		//	Behind it: whatever the bead could not take is smeared. Streaks run ALONG the way the hand
		//	went, so they vary across it - a couple of incommensurate waves a few millimetres apart,
		//	which is the spacing a squeegee leaves. A hash of the coordinate would be pixel noise.
		else if (d_mm <= -DA_VD_WIPE_W && d_mm > -DA_VD_WIPE_W - 14.0f)
		{
			const float across = mm_here.y;
			const float streak = 0.55f + 0.45f * saturate(0.5f + 0.35f * sin(across * 1.9f) +
				0.30f * sin(across * 0.73f + 2.1f) + 0.20f * sin(across * 4.3f + 0.7f));
			const float taken = h * (1.0f - DA_VD_WIPE_KEEP);
			h -= taken;
			film = min(film + taken * DA_VD_WIPE_SMEAR * streak, DA_VD_FILM_MAX * 3.0f);
			v *= 0.15f;
		}
	}

	//	---- what arrives -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	//	Rain. One lottery per cell per step, and the cell is bigger than the drops so two of them do
	//	not land in the same place. The 3x3 of cells around this texel is what can reach it.
	[branch]
	if (wet > 0.002f)
	{
		const float cell_uv = DA_VD_CELL_MM * uv_per_mm.x;
		const float p_cell = DA_VD_FLUX * wet * (DA_VD_CELL_MM * DA_VD_CELL_MM * 1e-6f) * dt;
		const int2 c0 = int2(floor(I.tc0 / cell_uv));
		[unroll]
		for (int j = -1; j <= 1; ++j)
		{
			[unroll]
			for (int i = -1; i <= 1; ++i)
			{
				const int2 cc = c0 + int2(i, j);
				[branch]
				if (da_vd_hash(cc, step_n) >= p_cell)
					continue;
				//	Where in the cell, and how big. The size distribution is skewed to the small ones -
				//	a cube of a uniform is close enough to a rain spectrum at this scale.
				const float rx = da_vd_hash(cc, step_n ^ 0x9e3779b9u);
				const float ry = da_vd_hash(cc, step_n ^ 0x85ebca6bu);
				const float rr = da_vd_hash(cc, step_n ^ 0xc2b2ae35u);
				const float rad = lerp(DA_VD_RAD_MIN, DA_VD_RAD_MAX, rr * rr * rr);
				const float2 at = (float2(cc) + float2(rx, ry)) * cell_uv;
				const float2 dmm = (I.tc0 - at) / max(uv_per_mm, 1e-6f);
				const float q = dot(dmm, dmm) / max(rad * rad, 1e-4f);
				[branch]
				if (q > 4.0f)
					continue;
				//	A spherical cap: the peak of an arriving drop is about half its radius.
				h += exp(-q) * rad * 0.5f;
			}
		}
	}

	//	A lens for the rig: a spherical cap of this radius, meeting the glass at forty-five
	//	degrees, held at the centre so the optics can be checked on something bigger than a bead.
	//	Held still - the water in it is not allowed to move, or the field tears something that
	//	tall into fingers within a second and there is no lens to look at.
	[branch]
	if (da_visor_wipe.w > 0.0f)
	{
		const float rad = da_visor_wipe.w;
		const float2 dmm = (I.tc0 - 0.5f) / max(uv_per_mm, 1e-6f);
		const float r2 = dot(dmm, dmm);
		[branch]
		if (r2 < rad * rad)
		{
			const float rs = rad * 1.4142f;
			h = max(h, sqrt(max(rs * rs - r2, 0.0f)) - (rs - rad * 0.4142f));
			v = (float2)0.0f;
		}
	}

	//	---- drying --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	//	Not while it rains. The air over a plate in the rain is saturated and nothing on it dries;
	//	the water goes by running off, by being wiped, or not at all - which is why a window in
	//	steady rain is COVERED. Dried at one rate whatever the weather, a small bead lasted twenty
	//	seconds and the glass lost its water everywhere, all the time, and the hand had no point.
	//	The film keeps a slow fade in the rain: a smear is buried by fresh drops in reality, and
	//	this stands in for that.
	const float dry_k = 1.0f - saturate(wet * 4.0f);
	h = max(h - DA_VD_DRY * dt * dry_k, 0.0f);
	film = max(film - DA_VD_DRY_FILM * dt * (0.25f + 0.75f * dry_k), 0.0f);

	h = min(h, DA_VD_CLAMP);
	film = min(film, DA_VD_FILM_MAX * 3.0f);
	//	Glass with no water in it holds no velocity, or the field keeps a ghost of every drop that ever
	//	crossed it and the next one to arrive is thrown sideways by it. The flux needs no velocity on
	//	a dry cell: a cell that has nothing sends nothing, and one that is about to receive does not
	//	have to know anything - the sender decides.
	v *= saturate(h * 20.0f);
	//	A cell that has gone non-finite is emptied rather than kept. It should not happen - every
	//	term above is bounded - but a NaN here is not one bad cell, it is every cell within reach
	//	of it a few steps later and then the whole glass, and max() and clamp() on most hardware
	//	quietly turn a NaN thickness into zero while leaving a NaN velocity at the clamp, which
	//	flushes every new drop off the glass the step it lands: a visor that is empty and stays
	//	empty however hard it rains. A comparison a NaN fails is the whole guard.
	const bool finite = (h < 1e6f) && (abs(v.x) + abs(v.y) < 1e6f) && (film < 1e6f);
	[flatten]
	if (!finite)
	{
		h = 0.0f;
		film = 0.0f;
		v = (float2)0.0f;
	}
	return float4(h, film, clamp(v, -4000.0f, 4000.0f));
}
